              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": null,
                "end_index": 132,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Because vitamin B12 is a water-soluble vitamin, your body generally absorbs what it needs and excretes the excess through your urine"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 134,
                "end_index": 398,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Because it has an exceptionally low risk of toxicity, major health organizations (such as the U.S. Food and Nutrition Board) **have not established a Tolerable Upper Intake Level (UL)**\u2014meaning there is no officially defined \"maximum safe limit\" for daily intake"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                2
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 402,
                "end_index": 553,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "However, taking excessively high doses is rarely necessary, and \"too much\" can still cause some minor side effects or point to underlying health issues"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 594,
                "end_index": 729,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **The Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA):** For most adults, the daily recommended amount of vitamin B12 is **2.4 micrograms (mcg)**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 732,
                "end_index": 913,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Why Supplement Doses Are So High:** You may notice that over-the-counter B12 supplements often contain 500 mcg, 1,000 mcg, or even 2,000 mcg (which is hundreds of times the RDA)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 915,
                "end_index": 1004,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This is because the body's absorption system is highly inefficient at processing oral B12"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5,
                0,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1082,
                "end_index": 1243,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Therefore, these high-dose supplements are generally considered safe and are often prescribed to treat deficiencies or absorption issues (like pernicious anemia)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                2
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1253,
                "end_index": 1465,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Side Effects of High-Dose B12\nWhile B12 is highly safe, taking extremely high doses\u2014particularly via prescribed intramuscular injections or very high-dose daily supplements\u2014can occasionally cause side effects"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6,
                2,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1253,
                "end_index": 1604,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Side Effects of High-Dose B12\nWhile B12 is highly safe, taking extremely high doses\u2014particularly via prescribed intramuscular injections or very high-dose daily supplements\u2014can occasionally cause side effects:\n* **Skin Breakouts:** The most common and well-documented side effect of high B12 levels is acne-like skin eruptions or rosacea flare-ups"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6,
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1606,
                "end_index": 1741,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Mild Physical Symptoms:** Some people report headaches, dizziness, or mild gastrointestinal discomfort (such as nausea or diarrhea)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1744,
                "end_index": 1887,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Rare Reactions:** In very rare cases, individuals receiving high-dose treatments have reported temporary anxiety, palpitations, or insomnia"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1919,
                "end_index": 2097,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "High Supplement Intake\nIt is important to distinguish between taking high-dose supplements and having high levels of B12 on a blood test (a condition called *hypercobalaminemia*)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4,
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2241,
                "end_index": 2335,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Discussing a lower maintenance dose with your doctor can help bring your levels back to normal"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2337,
                "end_index": 2466,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Without Supplements:** If your B12 blood levels are elevated and you **do not** take supplements, you should consult a doctor"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2468,
                "end_index": 2727,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "In these cases, high blood B12 is not caused by \"overdosing\" on food; instead, it can sometimes be a biological marker for an underlying medical condition (such as liver disease, kidney issues, or certain blood disorders) that requires professional evaluation"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2730,
                "end_index": 2878,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "### Next Steps\nIf you are currently taking B12 and are concerned about your dosage, the best approach is to ask your doctor for a routine blood test"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2880,
                "end_index": 3040,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "They can check your actual B12 levels and help you determine if you need to adjust your supplement intake based on your specific dietary needs and health status"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            }
